WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Good morning, John. Today I want to talk about saying stuff wrong.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.800
It is, as far as I am concerned, the most embarrassing thing that can happen

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.880
and unfortunately, just like when your fly is down,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:10.520
sometimes people are embarrassed about telling you about it

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.300
and so you go on and on with this intellectual fly down,

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.520
your underwear exposed, and toilet paper hanging out the back of your pants.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.140
Number one: You couldn't care less. If you could care less, then you do care.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.160
Number two: For all intents and purposes.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.100
I don't know what intensive purposes are, but that's not what you mean.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.240
Three: You do not hold down the fort, because the fort isn't inflatable and filled with helium.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.300
You hold the fort. It's a military metaphor.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.680
Five: This is Frankenstein? No.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.120
This is Frankenstein.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.900
That guy is Frankenstein's monster.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:53.300
Six: Dear Alanis Morissette, when you get a death row pardon two minutes too late,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.200
that is extremely unfortunate. It is not, however, ironic.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.380
And finally, number seven: If you're saying an acronym,

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.600
make sure that the thing you say after the acronym isn't in the acronym.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.600
Like when you put your PIN number in an ATM machine.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.380
Of course implying that you're putting your personal identification number number

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.560
into the automated teller machine machine.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.140
But I also want to note here that it's totally okay to say the La Brea tar pits,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.880
despite the fact that when you translate that out it's the the tar tar pits.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:22.440
Let's take a common comment on Vlogbrothers videos.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:26.160
You lose the game. Except that it is often spelled like this.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.540
In that case, I don't lose the game. Because I'm not thinking about the game.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.760
I'm thinking about the failure of the American education system.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.540
Moving on to the mother country, the United Kingdom,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:42.140
which is the United Kingdom of Northern Ireland and Great Britain.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.860
Great Britain is an island that contains three autonomous (ow) countries

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.800
the countries of Wales, Scotland, and England.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.900
So if you're in a pub in Wales, and you say "Gee guys, I sure love it here in England!",

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:59.480
they will punch you in the face. England is not a synonym for the United Kingdom.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.580
Now moving onto the two most confusing words in the English language,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.700
as far as I am concerned, affect and effect.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.320
Of course not pronounced ah-FECT and ee-FECT, but uh-FECT and uh-FECT.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.460
But I remember it as this way - the greenhouse effect affects the climate.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.280
Effect almost always being a noun and affect almost always being a verb.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.440
But this is extra, super confusing because sometimes effect is a verb, too.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:32.140
So it is literally okay to say "I effected the effect". That verb, effect, means to bring about an effect.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:36.460
So if you say "I effected the effect" you mean "I brought about the effect".

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:41.440
If you say "I affected the effect" that means that you changed the effect.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.180
so if you are good at bringing about effects, you are effective.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.740
Sorry, this is horribly confusing, it just is, but it's important to figure it out.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.240
Now to finish this off I am going to talk about some mispronunciation,

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.780
because when I was in college I did a long oral report on Leon Battista Alberti,

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:03.040
in which I discussed at length all the façades he designed in Renaissance Italy,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.600
except instead of saying fa-SAHD I said fa-KEHD,

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.320
and it was one of the most embarrassing moments of my life when I found out.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.160
Okay, let's go!

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.960
[Awry] It's not aw-REE, it's uh-RYE.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.560
[Epitome] It's ih-PIT-uh-mee, not eh-PEE-tome.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:17.780
[Edinburgh] E-din-bur-uh not E-din-burg.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.340
[Ethereal] Not EE-thuh-ree-ul but ee-THEE-ree-ul.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.480
[Meme] Not MEE-mee, but MEEM.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.680
[Haphazard] Not ha-FAZ-urd, but hap-HAZ-urd.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.060
[Facetious] fuh-SEE-shus, not FACE-chus.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.360
[Hyperbole] Not the sequel to the Super Bowl, the Hyper Bowl, but in fact hai-PER-buh-lee.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.360
[Colonel] KER-nel, despite the fact that this word has no r in it.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.100
[Boolean] BOO-lee-un not boo-LEEN.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.200
[Disheveled] And di-SHEV-uld not dis-HEEV-uld.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.480
And that is all I got for you. I hope that I have, at least a little bit,

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.280
helped the world avoid some embarrassment.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.160
Nerdfighteria, in the comments below, help me feel better about my façade mistake

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.340
and tell me some words that you've mispronounced in public before.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.760
John, I'll see you on Wednesday.

